# Vulnerability details

The `_convertDecimals` is to ensure the unwrapping of tokens have the correct conversion of tokens to the standard of 18 decimals. But the problem is that the whole logic is flawed. If the `decimalsTo` is less than `decimalsFrom` and the `amountToConvert` is not in 1e18, the returned amount is flawed. The developers assume that the users will always input 1e18 for conversion. When swapping or unwrapping the tokens, the tokens will be unwrapped in amount not specified by the user.


Let's say that the user want to wrap 1e12 USDCs we know that the `decimalsTo` is 18 as it's hardcoded, then `decimalsFrom` is 6 decimals. It will pass to the else statement which returns 0.000001. Instead of wrapping 1e12 USDC tokens, you get 0.000001 USDC.
```
// Ocean.sol::_convertDecimals
        } else {
            // Decimal shift right (remove precision) -> truncation
            // if less than 18 decimals
            uint256 shift = 10 ** (uint256(decimalsFrom - decimalsTo)); //12
            // @audit-issue
            // Lost of funds
            convertedAmount = amountToConvert / shift; // 1 will be returned 
            truncatedAmount = amountToConvert % shift; 
        }
```

Furthermore, if you pass `amountToConvert` and swap anything less than 1e12 with 6 decimals tokens, it reverts since the `inputAmount` parameter is 0 due to wrong business logic conversion. (You can test it, by changing `uint256 amount = 1e9;` to 1e6 in the PoC)

